<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>The Token Bottleneck</h2>
<p>Modern Large Language Models (LLMs) are bound to a strictly sequential, token-by-token generation interface. To execute chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning, the model externalizes its intermediate representations into discrete, human-readable words. However, natural language is a highly redundant and lossy communication channel for neural computation. This sequential decoding paradigm introduces significant inefficiencies:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Linguistic Redundancy:</strong> Models spend valuable compute generating articles, punctuation, and structural syntax (e.g., <em>"Therefore, to find the result, we first..."</em>) rather than performing semantic computation.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantization Bottleneck:</strong> Forcing a continuous 4096-dimensional model representation through a narrow vocabulary channel compresses and discards granular probabilities and soft intermediate thinking traces.</li>
  <li><strong>Decoding Overhead:</strong> Each reasoning step demands a full auto-regressive model forward pass and a memory-bound key-value (KV) cache write.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Interactive: Explicit vs. Latent Flow</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Compare the channels</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="toggle-btn-row">
      <button class="toggle-btn active" id="btn-flow-explicit">Explicit Verbal Channel</button>
      <button class="toggle-btn" id="btn-flow-latent">Continuous Latent Channel</button>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-diagram-container" id="flow-diagram">
      <div class="flow-step-box" style="border-color: var(--amber)">Input: "Next in sequence: 2, 4, 8, 16..."</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">↓ [Slow Sequential Output]</div>
      <div class="flow-step-box" style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.7rem;">"The pattern is doubling... So the next token should be..." (15 tokens generated)</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">↓ [Vocab Projection]</div>
      <div class="flow-step-box" style="border-color: var(--green)">Output: "32"</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The survey paper <em>"The Latent Space: Foundation, Evolution, Mechanism, Ability, and Outlook"</em> consolidates the rapidly expanding literature exploring <strong>Latent Space</strong> as a native substrate for model computation. Rather than projecting activations to discrete tokens, models learn to compute, plan, and communicate directly inside the continuous manifold of model hidden states.</p>

<blockquote class="callout">
  <div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
  <div class="callout-content">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Continuous latent reasoning moves LLM processing away from string-to-string generation and maps it to smooth trajectories in vector space, allowing parallel thought expansion and higher representational fidelity.
  </div>
</blockquote>
</section>

<section id="s-foundation" data-code="foundation">
<div class="section-no">02 — Foundation</div>
<h2>Foundation: Latent vs. Explicit</h2>
<p>To understand the paradigm shift, we must clarify what the latent space of a language-based model actually represents, and contrast it with traditional explicit models as well as the latent spaces of visual generative models (like VAEs or Diffusion Models).</p>

<p>For a standard language-based model, the <strong>explicit space</strong> is defined by a finite vocabulary $V$. In contrast, the <strong>latent space</strong> $H$ is the continuous, learned representational manifold of model hidden activations. The input sequence $x \in V$ is mapped to continuous embeddings, transformed through intermediate states, and projected back into the vocabulary space only when necessary.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Properties Comparison: Explicit vs. Latent Space</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:0">
    <table class="results-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Dimension</th>
          <th>Explicit Space (Verbal)</th>
          <th>Latent Space (Machine-Native)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Representation Format</strong></td>
          <td>Discrete symbols / subword tokens</td>
          <td>Continuous high-dimensional vectors ($R^d$)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Human Interpretability</strong></td>
          <td>High (readable text sequences)</td>
          <td>Low (non-trivial vector coordinates)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Information Density</strong></td>
          <td>Low (linguistic redundancy, filler words)</td>
          <td>High (dense semantic vectors, soft properties)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Differentiability</strong></td>
          <td>Non-differentiable (discrete sampling)</td>
          <td>Fully differentiable (backpropagation ready)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="highlight-row">
          <td><strong>Decoding Bottleneck</strong></td>
          <td>Sequential step-by-step auto-regression</td>
          <td>Recurrent loops, parallel Jacobi updates</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>LMs vs. Visual Generative Models</h3>
<p>While generative visual models (like Stable Diffusion) also utilize latent spaces, they differ fundamentally from LMs in their construction:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Objective:</strong> Visual model latents are trained using a reconstruction loss (anchoring them to local Euclidean properties for image patches), whereas LM hidden states are structured via a predictive next-token objective.</li>
  <li><strong>Structure:</strong> Visual latents carry spatial-temporal grid topologies. LM latents focus purely on linguistic semantics and abstract concepts, devoid of fixed spatial constraints.</li>
  <li><strong>Controllability:</strong> Visual models use cross-attention maps for spatial controls. LM latents leverage vector algebraic steering (e.g. concept adding/subtracting) along semantic paths.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s-taxonomy" data-code="taxonomy">
<div class="section-no">03 — Architecture & Representation</div>
<h2>Mechanism Taxonomy: Structuring Latents</h2>
<p>The survey organizes the technical landscape of latent space methods into four distinct mechanistic categories: **Architecture**, **Representation**, **Computation**, and **Optimization**.</p>

<div class="tech-grid">
  <div class="tech-card">
    <h4>1. Architecture</h4>
    <p>Determines where the latent system $H$ is structurally integrated: in the recurrent <strong>backbone</strong> layers, as a plug-in **component** (like memory/adapters), or guided via an **auxiliary model**.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tech-card">
    <h4>2. Representation</h4>
    <p>Defines the latent carrier $z$: **Internal** (readout activations), **External** (exogenous frozen features), **Learnable** (virtual concept tokens), or **Hybrid** configurations.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tech-card">
    <h4>3. Computation</h4>
    <p>Controls how activations are processed: **Compressed** (pooling states), **Expanded** (width/depth loop iterations), **Adaptive** (dynamic early halting), or **Interleaved**.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tech-card">
    <h4>4. Optimization</h4>
    <p>Specifies when the representation is learned: **Pre-training** from scratch, **Post-training** (alignment tuning), or **Inference-time** steering.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>In the math block below, we define the difference between standard autoregressive decoding and latent-conditioned generation:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Mathematical Formulation</div>
  <span class="math-comment"># Standard next-token generation</span>
  y_t ~ P(y_t | x, y_&lt;t)

  <span class="math-comment"># Latent-conditioned model generation</span>
  z = \Phi_comp(h, x)      <span class="math-comment"># Latent carrier z derived from states h</span>
  y ~ \Phi_back(· | x, z)   <span class="math-comment"># Output conditioned on continuous z</span>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-computation" data-code="computation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Looped & Adaptive Refinement</div>
<h2>Computation: Looped & Adaptive Refinement</h2>
<p>A major focus of latent space research is <strong>recurrent looping</strong>. In standard transformers, the computational depth is fixed by the layer stack (e.g., a token passes through 32 distinct layers). In parameter-shared looped backbones (such as <em>Huginn</em> or <em>Ouro</em>), a smaller set of layers is applied repeatedly (e.g. $N$ times) to refine the hidden state trajectory.</p>

<p>This recurrent computation enables <strong>Test-Time Compute Scaling</strong>: by scaling the number of loop iterations $N$ during inference, the model can dedicate more computational steps to complex tasks, allowing states to converge smoothly to target concepts.</p>

