<p>Furthermore, <strong>Adaptive Halting</strong> techniques predict a halt probability at each iteration. If a question is simple (e.g. <em>"What is 2+2?"</em>), the model halts at $N=1$. For complex reasoning (e.g. multi-step deduction), the model dynamically continues looping, optimizing latency while maintaining deep reasoning capacity.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-abilities" data-code="abilities">
<div class="section-no">05 — Downstream Abilities</div>
<h2>Downstream Abilities: Reasoning & Planning</h2>
<p>Moving representations into continuous manifolds unlocks a broad spectrum of capabilities. A prominent example is <strong>implicit latent reasoning</strong>, popularized by models like <strong>COCONUT</strong> (Continuous Chain of Thought). Instead of generating textual thought steps, COCONUT trains the model to predict next-step activations in latent space, replacing discrete intermediate tokens with continuous thought vectors.</p>

<p>Let's navigate the sequence of a latent reasoning step in models like COCONUT:</p>

